  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A Nor'easter brings below normal temperatures,\nbreezy conditions, and some rain at times through this weekend.\n\nA band of light rain has been spiraling back into our area\naround the western side of the Nor'easter. Additional light rain\nor showers will move in through the remainder of the afternoon\ninto the early evening, but as showers progress westward they\nwill tend to evaporate as they hit much drier air mass west of\nthe Blue Ridge Mountains. \n\nAs the Nor'easter reaches its northern most position late\ntonight, there will be a break in the rain late this evening\nthrough daybreak Sunday. \n\nAs the low center circles back into our area tomorrow, expect\nmore rain late tomorrow morning through the day Sunday. Chances\nwill be highest across the northeast part of the fcst area, and\nthis period has the highest chance for rain to reach as far \nwest as the Alleghenies. Northeast Maryland could see up to an\ninch of rain through the day Sunday.\n\nWind gusts will be 20-35 mph today, and up to 40 mph near the \nChesapeake Bay. Similar or slightly lower winds are likely on \nSunday.\n\nWhile rain chances and wind gusts will likely start declining \nby Monday, cloud cover will likely remain stubbornly in place. \nThe current forecast calls for upper 60s and lower 70s, but \nwould not be surprised if they are a bit lower if clouds are \nthick. As winds shift more northwesterly, some upslope showers \nor drizzle will be possible in the Alleghenies.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Generally warm and benign weather next week before rainfall \nchances return by next weekend.\n\nWhile rain chances and wind gusts decline on Monday, cloud cover \nremains stubborn, potentially limiting sunshine to start the week. \nOur current forecast calls for upper 60s and lower 70s, but could \ntrend a bit cooler if a thicker cloud deck persists. As \nnorthwesterly wind shifts slightly further west, some upslope \nshowers or drizzle will be possible in the Alleghenies.\n\nContinuing into the beginning of next week, an upper level ridge \nadvances overhead from the west, building high pressure at the \nsurface through Wednesday. Overall, this pattern favors dry and \ngradually warmer conditions with highs in the 70s potentially \ncreeping up into the low 80s late next week. One potential caveat \ncould be Wednesday night into Thursday as a fast moving Clipper \nsystem passes by to our north. Given all of the forcing remains to \nthe north and moisture return ahead of the system is weak, showers \nand thunderstorm potential appear negligible. The next chance for \nrain looks to be Friday and into next weekend ahead of an \napproaching cold front from Canada.",
